{"kind":"request","text":"Show interpretation warnings in context instead of as a room-level alarm. Hugh reported the Interpretation panel reading as a functional issue when both entries were benign: two inert historical acts and one known design limit with a request already open. A warning surfaced away from what it describes, that names a count rather than a consequence, trains readers to ignore it.","body":{"conditions":"Move interpretation gaps to the act they concern. An act the room cannot read should say so on that act, where a reader looking at it can see why it carries no force - undefined kind, the kind as written, and what that means for anything resting on it. Remove or demote the aggregate panel that counts them, which reports a number a reader cannot act on and reads as a system fault. Treat the unbound fold separately: it is a standing property of this room, not an incident, so state it where the room's interpretive scope is relevant and in the register used for the loopback boundary - a stated limit, not an error. Judge by whether a reader can tell, without leaving the surface, whether anything is owed: the two acts at tickets 97 and 98 have withdrawn commitments and merged heads, so nothing is, and the display should not imply otherwise. Do not solve this by hiding interpretation gaps altogether - a live gap on an act someone is waiting on must still be visible on that act. Add a rendered component test that fails if an undefined-kind act renders without its explanation, and pin whatever replaces the panel.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}